Z||000001||Haze_Performance||11-03-2000||04:45 AM||||<B>I think that you should sell that car ASAP!!! When the washer pump goes out it wont be long before the engine goes... </B><P>I will take it off your hands for a very fair price of $100.00.<P>Ill be over at 7:30pm Sat. to pick it up.<P>(That gives me a half hour to stop at Trac Auto and pick up a new $6.00 washer pump  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||prof bobo||11-03-2000||11:03 AM||||The OEM pump is a piece of fertilizer.  I took mine apart and cleaned the corrosion out of it a couple of times, finally got disgusted with it and threw it away.  I got one of those 'universal' replacement types where the pump is inline with the hose instead of stuck on the tank, siliconed the tubing into the tank, mounted the pump on the frame and it worked without problems for years.||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||11-03-2000||12:39 PM||||I mentioned this before: that if you have delay wipers the switch for the pump is routed through the motor so you have the 4 wipes after fluid is applied "feature". Since<BR>your pump is tested good on another car you may have a problem with the circuit board on the back of the motor or the switch in the steering column. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||11-04-2000||01:42 AM||||this happened with my car, and i grabbed one from the junkyard and it worked.<P>One thing you can try to, is to  get someone to turn it on, while your at the other end with a circuit tester, and see if the light comes on, you know it has to be the motor.||||reg||1
